  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_007 (scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted) is the strongest configuration at 0.706 acc / 0.667 F1, clearly outperforming both the scgpt-based multimodal models (~0.51-0.54) and the scvi_n200 RNA-only baseline (0.650). All completed experiments use avgpool aggregation; transformer aggregation has not been tested on any configuration. Question: Does replacing avgpool with transformer aggregation improve performance on the best-performing setup, or is simple averaging sufficient? This is a single-variable change (avgpool → transformer) from config_007, cleanly isolating the aggregation mechanism while holding RNA encoder, image encoder, profile setting, and fusion constant. Rationale: Aggregation determines how multiprofile information is combined, a foundational design choice. If transformer aggregation helps, it generalizes to other encoder combinations; if it does not, we can rule out that axis for the scvi_n200 family and focus remaining budget on fusion or image-encoder comparisons. This is the highest-information single step to exploit the strong scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l signal."
}

